{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Its fat, the whole tail.</b> In the sacred language, חלב is the fat that separates from the flesh and is peeled away from it; for the fat that is intermingled with the flesh and does not separate from it is called שומן, as it is written (Deuteronomy 32:15) ״וישמן ישרון״, ״שמנת עבית״, (Psalms 63:6) ״כמו חלב ודשן תשבע נפשי״. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory said concerning this fat that separates from the flesh: it is a membrane patch that is peeled away, and they called it by this name because it is a garment (לבוש) in which the flesh is clothed, like a כשמלה — whose Aramaic translation of שמלתו is תותביה; and it is a membrane and it is easy to peel. From this you learn that the two are not equal, neither in their name nor in their nature — not in their name, for the fat that separates and is peeled from the flesh is called חלב, and that which does not separate but is intermingled with the flesh is called שומן; and not in their nature, for the fat that separates is cold and moist and has coarseness and thickness, difficult to digest in the stomach (אסטומכא), whereas the fat intermingled with the flesh is hot and moist." "<b>And hence</b> one should ask: since the fat that separates is called חלב in the sacred language, how can the verse say ״חלבו האליה תמימה״? For surely there is no חלב in the tail at all — it is entirely שומן intermingled there, which does not separate from it. And if we say that the meaning of ״חלבו האליה״ is 'its שומן,' what shall we say of the verse ״כל חלב וכל דם לא תאכלו״? For surely if שומן were called חלב, we would have to forbid all the שומן in animals, and nothing would be permitted to eat but the red flesh alone. Therefore one must interpret ״חלבו האליה תמימה״ that the intent is not about שומן but about חלב. And if you say, what חלב is there in the tail? — thus the verse was interpreted: ״חלבו האליה״ means 'its חלב together with the tail,' to say that he shall offer from it his חלב in a whole tail, and the intent is that when he removes the whole tail with the backbone (עצה), much חלב is removed with it, for it is intermingled with it on the inner side." "<b>And thus</b> the Gaon Rabbeinu Saadya of blessed memory explained that the meaning of ״חלבו האליה״ is as if it said ״חלבו והאליה״, in the manner of what is written (1 Chronicles 1:1) ״אדם שת אנוש״ — its sense being 'שת ואנוש'; and so (Exodus 39:33) ״קרסיו קרשיו בריחיו ועמודיו ואדניו״; and so (Leviticus 7:37) ״לעולה למנחה״ — its sense being ״ולמנחה״, for the burnt-offering is not a meal-offering; and so one cannot say that Adam is Shet Enosh, and likewise here one cannot say that the tail is the חלב." "<b>Close to the backbone he shall remove it.</b> The word עצה embraces both the sense of עץ and the sense of עצה — from the sense of עץ, for it is the extremity of the spine [and resembles a tree and its branches], and so its Aramaic translation is שדרתא; and from the sense of עצה, for he removes it above the kidneys that deliberate (היועצות), and this is why it says ״על הכליות יסירנה״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {2 "Rabbeinu Bahya cites 1 Chronicles 1:1 as illustration of asyndetic listing read with implied conjunction; the Adam–Shet–Enosh example mirrors the construct-chain ambiguity he resolves."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:22:45.998210264Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}